version: 2
title: How a views document works
description: One list of items in the file and the views over it — the views the file names, each over the items its filter keeps, or one of every kind the roles allow; a table, a kanban, a calendar by day, week or month, a gantt nested by parent, a sequence — the gantt's rows on steps of what comes after what, not days — a dependency tree, and a page the view's own Nunjucks template renders; an item may hold one document of another kind, drawn in its dialog; read only, nothing referenced, the open view kept for the session.
events:
  - key: always
    label: Always
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Always
    detail: What holds at every moment — the shape of the file, how the roles allow views and the file picks them, and where the engine lives.
    content:
      key: shape
      label: The shape of the file
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The shape of the file
        description: The items are the data; the roles say what each key means; the views say how to look.
        sections:
          - title: Top-level keys
            body: |
              | key | what it is |
              |---|---|
              | `title` | the heading |
              | `description` | one line under it |
              | `fields` | which item key plays which role — `id`, `title`, `status`, `start`, `end`, `previous`, `parent` |
              | `columns` | the statuses in order, for the kanban; absent = the values found, first seen first |
              | `views` | the views to offer, in order, each over the items its filter keeps; absent = one of every kind the roles allow |
              | `items` | the list itself — mappings, each with an id |

              ```yaml
              title: Launch
              fields:
                id: id
                title: title
                status: status
                start: start
                end: end
                previous: after
                parent: under
              columns: [To do, Doing, Done]
              views:
                - key: open
                  kind: kanban
                  label: Open work
                  filter: [{field: status, op: not_equals, value: Done}]
                  group: owner
                - key: plan
                  kind: gantt
                - key: steps
                  kind: sequence
              items:
                - id: plan
                  title: Plan the launch
                  status: Done
                  start: 2026-10-01
                  end: 2026-10-03
                - id: build
                  title: Build it
                  status: Doing
                  after: plan
                - id: api
                  title: The API
                  status: Doing
                  start: 2026-10-04
                  end: 2026-10-07
                  under: build
                - id: demo
                  title: Demo the API
                  status: To do
                  start: 2026-10-07 14:00
                  end: 2026-10-07 15:30
                  after: api
              ```

              An item's keys are its own — anything beside the roles is shown as it is, but
              `content`, a document written into the item. A start or an end is a day,
              `YYYY-MM-DD`, or a time on it, `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM` — a `T` or a space between, a
              24-hour clock and no zone: the time the calendar shows; bare or quoted. An item with a
              time at either end is timed — a start with no time is the start of its day, an end with
              none the end of its day, and a start with a time and no end is a moment — and an item
              of days alone is all-day. `previous` is one id or a list of ids, `parent` one id,
              every one an item of this file. `id` and `title` default to keys of those names; an
              item with no id is given `#N` so the rest still draws, and a second item with an id
              already seen is dropped.
          - title: The roles allow views
            body: |
              | view | needs |
              |---|---|
              | Table | nothing — always allowed |
              | Kanban | `status` |
              | Calendar | `start` (`end` when there is one; without, the start's day, or its moment when it has a time) |
              | Gantt | `start`, `end`, and `previous` or `parent` |
              | Sequence | `previous` or `parent` |
              | Tree | `previous` |
              | Page | nothing — but only a view the file names, with its `template`, is one |

              A view whose roles the file does not name cannot be offered — there is no empty
              kanban to click into.
          - title: The file picks its views
            body: |
              `views` is a list, each `key` (its identity), `kind` (`table`, `kanban`, `calendar`,
              `gantt`, `sequence`, `tree`, `page`), an optional `label` (the button's text; the kind's name when absent),
              and the table rules every kind shares: `filter` — clauses over the items' own keys,
              `{field, op, value}` with the one vocabulary (`equals`, `not_equals`, `contains`,
              `not_contains`, `starts_with`, `ends_with`, `matches`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`,
              `between`, `in`, `not_in`, `is_empty`, `not_empty`, `is_true`, `is_false`; one value
              or a list), every one of which must hold — `sort` (`{field, dir}`, first key first) and
              `limit`. The rules read an item's keys as the table shows them — a date as its day,
              with its time when it has one — so a day and the times on it sort together, the day
              first. A filter hides items for that view only; an edge to a hidden item is dropped
              for that view. A KANBAN, a GANTT or a SEQUENCE view takes one key of its own, `group` —
              the item key whose values lane the board, or gather the outline's rows (see *A grouped
              kanban* and *A grouped gantt or sequence*). Two views of one kind are two ways of
              looking — `open`, a kanban of what is not Done; `mine`, a kanban of one owner's.
              Without `views`, one view of every kind the roles allow is offered, the table first.
          - title: A grouped kanban
            body: |
              A kanban view may name a `group`: an item key whose values become the board's LANES —
              rows of the board, the status columns running across each.

              ```yaml
              views:
                - key: board
                  kind: kanban
                  group: owner
              ```

              The lanes are the values that key takes among the view's items, in the order they are
              first seen, and a last lane, unlabelled, for the items carrying no value for it. They
              are DERIVED from the items and never listed — a lane is a value that is there, so a
              lane cannot go stale against the items the way a written list would. A `group` naming
              a key no item carries is a problem, as is a `group` on any kind but a kanban, a gantt
              or a sequence.

              This is the one axis a `.views` kanban has that a `.kanban` board does not: a
              `.kanban`'s rows are dependency depth, computed from `needs:`, and answer a different
              question — what can be started — where lanes answer whose work it is.
          - title: A grouped gantt or sequence
            body: |
              A gantt or a sequence view may name a `group` too: the rows at the top of its outline
              gathered under a row of their own per value of that key — the same values, in the same
              order, as a kanban's lanes, and a last row, "—", for the rows carrying none.

              ```yaml
              views:
                - key: steps
                  kind: sequence
                  group: source
              ```

              A group's row shows its value and how many rows it gathers, and a thin bar over their
              span; it collapses and expands as a parent does — its chevron, or its label, since a
              group has no item to open — and an arrow to or from a row it hides is drawn to or from
              it. Only the top rows are gathered: a part stays under its whole, whatever it carries.
              Like lanes, the groups are DERIVED from the items and never listed, and nothing about
              an item changes — not its step, not its dates.
          - title: A page view
            body: |
              A view the file names with `kind: page` and a `template` of its own — Nunjucks, as a
              `.page` writes it — and the `partials` its `include`, `import`, `from` and `extends`
              name. The template is handed:

              | variable | what it is |
              |---|---|
              | `items` | the view's items after its filter, sort and limit, each a mapping of its own keys as written — a date as `YYYY-MM-DD` |
              | `title` | the document's title |
              | `fields` | the role → key map, defaults filled — `item[fields.title]` is an item's label whatever its key |
              | `columns` | the kanban's columns: the file's, else the statuses found |
              | `view` | the view's `key` and `label` |

              ```yaml
              views:
                - key: report
                  kind: page
                  label: Open work
                  filter: [{field: status, op: not_equals, value: Done}]
                  partials:
                    row: <tr><td>{{ item.title }}</td><td>{{ item.status }}</td></tr>
                  template: |
                    <h1>{{ title }} — {{ view.label }}</h1>
                    <table>{% for item in items %}{% include "row" %}{% endfor %}</table>
              ```

              The template, the partials and the items are all in the file, so the page is as
              computable from the text as every other view.
          - title: A document written in
            body: |
              An item that holds more than its line shows writes that document into itself — the
              shape a brief's section and a kanban's task carry:

              ```yaml
              items:
                - id: kickoff
                  title: Kickoff
                  start: 2026-10-05
                  content:                   # one whole document, written into the item
                    kind: brief              # any kind the Studio knows — md, brief, playbook, …
                    doc:
                      title: The kickoff
                      sections:
                        - {title: Agenda, body: Scope, owners, the date.}
                - id: review
                  title: Review
                  start: 2026-10-12
                  content: {kind: md, doc: "The notes from the review."}
              ```

              `kind` names the engine that renders `doc`; `doc` is the document exactly as a file of
              that kind would hold it — its YAML, or its text for `md`. One document per item: an
              item that needs two is two items. It is the one key not shown as it is: the item's
              dialog draws it by its own kind, the table names its kind, and the kanban, the
              calendar, the gantt, the sequence and the tree mark the item beside its label. The checker runs it
              through its kind's engine, so a broken brief inside an item is a broken views document.
          - title: Parents and what comes before
            body: |
              Two different edges. `previous` is order — what has to be done before this — and it
              is what the tree and the arrows of the gantt and the sequence draw. `parent` is
              composition — the item this one is part of — and it is what the gantt and the sequence
              nest by: a child sits indented under its parent, and a parent with no dates of its own
              is drawn in the gantt as a thin bar over its children's span. An item can have both: a
              child that comes after its sibling.

              The sequence reads the two together, since it has no dates to say where a whole
              stands: a whole spans its parts, so what a whole comes after holds for every part,
              and what comes after a whole comes after all of its parts. That makes some edges
              impossible — a part after its own whole, or after what comes after it, and a whole
              after its own part — and the checker names them.
          - title: Why one file, read only
            body: |
              The items, the roles, the views and the documents items hold are in the file, so any
              view is computable from the text alone: paste it, check it, render it with nothing else
              on disk. The views read the
              items and write nothing — the file is edited as text, and every view follows on the
              next read — and which view is open is the page's for the session, never saved.
          - title: Where the engine lives
            body: |
              `python/studio_kinds/kinds/views.py` — `parse`, `read_fields`, `view_offered`,
              `available_views`, `rows_of_view`, `columns_of`, `layers`, `parent_cycle`, `unplaced`,
              `problems`, `summary`, with the clauses, the sort and the limit in `_rows.py` (`read_table_rules`,
              `apply_table`), and an item's document read, judged and checked by `read_content`,
              `content_problems` and `written_doc_problems` in `kinds/__init__.py`, as a brief's
              section's and a kanban's task's are; its header comment is what `studio-check --spec
              views` prints. The shape is `kinds/views/v1.schema.json` and the field table
              `v1.fields.yaml`. The view is `components/views/ViewsView.tsx` over `lib/viewsDoc.ts` —
              the table, the kanban, the gantt (`ganttOf`) and the sequence (`sequenceOf`) drawn by
              one outline chart that folds (`foldOutline`, `linksOf`), and the tree, in `panes.tsx`;
              the calendar in `CalendarPane.tsx`, loaded when a calendar first opens — FullCalendar's
              time grid for the week and the day and its day grid for the month, themed on the
              Studio's tokens by `components/calendar/EventCalendarViews.tsx` (FullCalendar's own
              shadcn theme), over the entries `calendarEntryOf` makes of the items and moved by
              `shiftCalendar`, `sameSpan` and `spanTitle` — with `ItemDialog.tsx`, which draws an item's document
              through `components/WrittenDocumentView.tsx`; the rules are `tablePolicy.ts`. A page
              view's model is `pageOfView` in `viewsDoc.ts`, and it renders in
              `components/page/PageFrame.tsx`, the sandboxed frame a `.page` renders in; its template
              and partials are checked by `template_problems` in `python/studio_kinds/kinds/page.py`.
  - key: opened
    label: The file is opened
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Opening
    detail: parseViews reads the YAML once and never throws; the views are offered; the first opens.
    content:
      key: drawn
      label: What is parsed and drawn
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What is parsed and drawn
        description: The parse, the derived facts, and the page.
        sections:
          - title: The parse
            body: |
              The title, the description, the roles (defaults for `id` and `title`), the columns
              when the file has them, the views when it names them — a view without a key or a
              kind, with a key already seen, a kind that is not one of the seven, or a kind whose
              roles are not named is left out, and a page view keeps its template (empty when
              absent) and its partials that are text — and every item: its id (a string, or a whole number
              as text), its label (the title key, else the id), its status, its start and end when
              they are days, each with the time on it when there is one, what it comes after and what it is part of — only ids that are items
              here, the item itself excluded — its document when `content` is a mapping (its kind as
              the Studio knows it: `.Brief` is `brief`), and all of its fields as written.
          - title: Derived
            body: |
              The kanban's columns: the file's, else every status found, first seen first. Each
              view's items: its filter, sort and limit over the items' own fields. The dependency
              depth of every item by longest chain of `previous` (Kahn's order; items in a cycle have
              none). The gantt's rows: nested by `parent`, a parent without dates spanning its
              children, and the day range from the earliest start to the latest end. The sequence's
              rows: nested by `parent`, each item's step one past the last of what it comes after and
              no earlier than its parent's, a parent spanning its parts' steps, and the items no step
              can hold. Nothing derived is written down.
          - title: What is drawn
            body: |
              The title and the description, and a row of view buttons — the file's views by their
              labels, else Table, Kanban, Calendar, Gantt, Sequence, Tree as the roles allow — the open one
              filled, each titled with its kind and what its rules do ("2 filters · sorted by start
              asc"). The first opens. At the right of the row, `N items`, or `N of M items` when the
              view's filter hides some, and the rules in words. Under the buttons, the view — a page
              view and a calendar filling what is left of the pane.
  - key: view-clicked
    label: A view button is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The pane shows the view's items that way; nothing is written; the choice lasts the session.
    content:
      key: views
      label: The seven kinds of view
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The seven kinds of view
        description: One pane, seven ways to lay the same items out — each over the items the view keeps. The kanban, the calendar, the gantt, the sequence and the tree mark an item that holds a document beside its label.
        sections:
          - title: Table
            body: |
              Every key any item carries, first seen first, as columns — the id and the label first
              — and one row per item in the view's order. A cell shows text as it is, a date as
              `YYYY-MM-DD` — `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM` when it has a time — a list joined by `, `, a mapping as JSON, and an item's document as its
              kind (`brief`, `md`). A row opens the item.
          - title: Kanban
            body: |
              The columns across, each with its count; under each, a card per item whose status
              matches it (case-insensitive), in the view's order; an item with no status, or a
              status no column spells, lands in the first column. A card shows the label and, when
              the roles allow, the dates and "⇠ N" for what it comes after. A card opens the item.
              A view with a `group` draws one LANE per value of that key, each a row of the board
              under its own heading with the count of what is in it, the columns running across
              every lane alike; the items with no value for the key make a last, unlabelled lane.
          - title: Calendar
            body: |
              A day, its week or its month — Day, Week and Month on the right of the header; the
              scale and the day are the page's for the session, per view, like the view itself. It
              opens at the earliest start: on Week when an item has a time, on Month otherwise. ‹
              and › move a day, a week or a month (a month keeps the day, its last when the month is
              shorter); "Today" jumps to today and "First item" to the earliest start. The header
              names the span: "October 2026", "5 – 11 October 2026", "Monday 5 October 2026". The
              calendar fills what is left of the pane.

              - **Week** — the seven days of the day's week across, Monday to Sunday, and the hours
                of the day down the side, as a diary is. An item with a time is a block from its
                start to its end, placed by the clock — items at the same hours side by side — and
                a moment is half an hour tall; an item of days alone sits in the all-day row above
                the hours, one bar across the days it covers, and so does an item with a time that
                lasts a day or more — a stay, a trip — its start time before its label. A line
                marks the time now on today. The hours scroll inside the calendar, opening an hour
                before the earliest time in the file (08:00 when none has one).
              - **Day** — the same, for one day.
              - **Month** — a grid of weeks, Monday to Sunday, the days of the months either side
                muted: an item of days alone a bar across the days it covers, an item with a time a
                line on its day with the time before the label. A day with more than fits shows
                "+N more", which lists that day's items.

              Every block, bar and line is coloured by its column when the file has a status,
              carries the mark of a document the item holds, and is titled "<label> · <start> →
              <end>"; a day's heading or number opens that day. Times read as the file writes them,
              on a 24-hour clock, whatever the zone of the machine. Items with no start date are
              listed under the calendar. A block, a bar or a line opens the item.
          - title: Gantt
            body: |
              One row per item with dates — its own, or its children's for a parent without — nested
              by `parent`: a child indented under its parent, siblings in dependency order (depth,
              then start, then the file's order). A day scale across the top — every day, the month
              named where it begins — and a bar per row from its start to its end, coloured by its
              column, the label on the left; a parent without dates gets a thin bar over its
              children's span. An arrow from the end of every `previous` item to the start of the one
              that comes after it. Items with no dates anywhere are listed under the chart. A bar
              opens the item.

              The labels are an outline, as a brief's tree is: a parent's chevron is its own target
              and only collapses its rows and expands them again, the label opens the item.
              Collapsed, a parent keeps its bar, and an arrow to or from a row it hides is drawn to
              or from the parent — the arrows between two of its own rows are not drawn. "Collapse
              all" and "Expand all" sit over the chart when a row has rows under it. Which parents
              are collapsed is the page's for the session, per view; everything opens expanded. A
              view with a `group` gathers its top rows under a row per value, which collapses the
              same way (see *A grouped gantt or sequence*).
          - title: Sequence
            body: |
              The gantt's rows on a scale of STEPS, not days — what comes after what, whatever the
              dates or how long anything takes. Every item takes one step and stands one step past
              the last step of everything it comes after, and no earlier than its parent may start;
              a parent spans its parts' steps, as a thin bar over them, so what a whole comes after
              holds for every part, and what comes after a whole comes after all of them. The scale
              across counts the steps from 1; an item that comes after nothing stands at step 1.

              Rows nested by `parent`, a part indented under its whole; siblings in the view's order,
              each below the siblings it comes after. The outline collapses and expands as
              the gantt's does, and the arrows run from the end of every `previous` item to the start
              of the one after it — always forward, since an item stands past what it comes after.
              Items no step can hold — caught in a loop of `previous`, of `parent`, or a part and its
              whole out of order — are listed under the chart. A bar opens the item. A view with a
              `group` gathers its top rows under a row per value, as the gantt's does.
          - title: Tree
            body: |
              What comes first at the top: every item that names no previous is a root; under each,
              the items that come after it, indented, and so on. An item that comes after two things
              appears under each, marked "also after <the other>". Items caught in a cycle are listed
              under the tree, since they have no root. A node opens the item.
          - title: Page
            body: |
              The view's template rendered with its model — the view's items, the title, the fields,
              the columns and the view — in the sandboxed frame a `.page` renders in: scripts
              allowed, the host's origin withheld, filling the pane under the buttons, white until
              the page paints a background of its own. A template the engine refuses shows its
              message in the frame's place, "The page did not render", until the text renders. What
              the page draws is the template's; nothing in it opens the item dialog.
  - key: item-clicked
    label: An item is clicked
    trigger: chosen
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The item dialog — the document written into the item, every field as written, and every edge as a jump.
    content:
      key: dialog
      label: What opens
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The label, then a line of facts: the status as a chip, the dates as `start → end` with their times, "after"
        with one chip per previous item, "followed by" with one per item that comes after, "part of"
        with the parent, "made of" with the children — a click JUMPS the dialog to that item, whether
        or not the open view shows it. Then, when the item holds a document, that document, drawn by
        its own kind's viewer from the text in this file — a brief's sections beside their tree,
        markdown rendered, a board or a book in a box of its own; nothing is read from disk, so it
        draws at once and carries no annotations, and the dialog widens for it. Then every other
        field of the item, as written, in a two-column list — the id with a copy button. The dialog
        stays on its id across a re-read and closes if the id is gone.
  - key: changed-on-disk
    label: The file changes on disk
    trigger: imposed
    arity: many
    domain: Reading
    detail: The host re-reads; the open view redraws from the new text; the open item stays if its id survived.
    content:
      key: reread
      label: What is re-read
      kind: md
      doc: |
        The desktop watcher (250 ms debounce, `studio:fs-changed`), the web folder entry's server
        events or VS Code's text document deliver the new text, and the Studio re-reads unless its
        autosave is dirty, saving or in error. Everything drawn is in the text — the items, the roles,
        the views, the columns — so the open view redraws at once; a view the new text no longer
        offers falls back to the first.
  - key: checked
    label: studio-check runs on it
    trigger: imposed
    domain: Writing
    detail: One file in, one verdict out — the parser never refuses, so the checker names everything it silently dropped or defaulted.
    content:
      key: checker
      label: What the checker does
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What the checker does
        description: The order it runs in, and what it leaves alone.
        sections:
          - title: In order
            body: |
              1. Parse the YAML — a parse error is the only problem reported.
              2. `problems`: not a mapping; no `title`; `fields` that is not a mapping, a role that
                 is not one of the seven, a role mapped to something that is not a key name;
                 `columns` that is not a list, an entry that is not text, or columns without a
                 `status` role; `views` that is not a list, a view that is not a mapping, without a
                 `key` or a `kind`, a key twice, a kind that is not one of the seven, a kind whose
                 roles are not named ("view open: a kanban needs a `status` role under `fields`"),
                 a page view with no `template`, one not text or blank, `partials` that are not a
                 mapping or a partial that is not text, a template naming a partial the view does
                 not hold ("view report: template: `include "row"` names no partial of this view
                 — write it under `partials`"), a `template` or `partials` on a view that is not a
                 page, a `group` on a view that is not a kanban, a gantt or a sequence, one that is
                 not an item key, or one no item carries, and its rules' own problems — a clause with no field or no op, an unknown op, a
                 sort direction that is not asc or desc, a limit that is not positive; no `items`
                 (`items: []` is an empty list and passes); an item that is not a mapping or has no
                 id; an id used twice; a label that is not text; a status that is not text, or not a
                 column when columns are given; a start or end that is not a date; a time after its
                 day the calendar cannot read ("item late: `from` has a time the calendar cannot
                 read — …"); a bare timestamp written in a zone, which the calendar reads in UTC
                 and so shows at another time; an end before its start, a day's end being its
                 whole day; a `previous` that is not an id or a list of ids, an empty entry, the item
                 itself, or an id no item has; a `parent` that is not an id, the item itself, or an
                 id no item has; a cycle of `previous`; a cycle of `parent`; when neither, a part and
                 its whole out of order — a part after its own whole or after what comes after it, a
                 whole after its own part, named with every item they leave without a step ("part and
                 whole out of order: build → api — …"); and what an item's
                 `content` cannot be — not a mapping, no `kind` or no `doc`, an `md` document that is
                 not a string, `by`, `docs` or `file` on it. Each is one line, naming the view or the
                 item.
              3. Every item's document through its own kind's engine, its problems prefixed with the
                 item and the kind ("item kickoff (brief): section Agenda: …"); a kind the Studio does
                 not know is one line.
              4. The summary line: `ok <name>  Views  12 items · table, kanban (3 columns), calendar,
                 gantt, sequence, tree` when the roles decide, or `12 items · views: open (kanban, 3 columns)
                 4, plan (gantt)` when the file names its views — a count after a view that hides
                 some, and `12 items, 2 documents` when items hold documents — so a misspelled role
                 or a filter that hides everything is visible at once.
          - title: Not checked
            body: |
              Whether an item's dates overlap its previous item's — the gantt shows it — or two
              items' times overlap — the week lays them side by side. Whether every
              item has a status, a start, a previous or a parent — an item without lands in the first
              column, under the grid, at a root, at the first step, or at the top. Whether a filter names a key any item
              carries — a clause over nothing hides everything, and the summary's count says so. The
              checker sees the file alone, which is all there is.
  - key: authored
    label: A views document is written
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Writing
    content:
      key: write
      label: Writing one
      kind: guide
      doc:
        title: Write a views document
        description: The items, the roles that allow views, the views that pick them.
        steps:
          - key: template
            label: Start from the template
            detail: |
              `studio-check --template views > new.views` — five items with every role named and
              six views, one of each kind but the page, the kanban filtered to what is not Done; replace them.
          - key: items
            label: Write the items with ids
            after: [template]
            detail: |
              One mapping per item, the id key first, then whatever the item is — a name, a
              status, dates, an owner, a note. Keep ids stable: `previous` and `parent` point at them.
          - key: content
            label: Write in what an item has to open
            after: [items]
            detail: |
              An item that holds more than its line shows — a meeting's agenda, a review's notes —
              takes `content: {kind, doc}`: `kind` any kind the Studio knows, `doc` that kind's
              YAML, a block string for `md`. Its dialog draws it, and the checker runs it through
              that kind's engine. One document per item: an item that needs two is two items.
          - key: roles
            label: Say which keys play which role
            after: [items]
            detail: |
              Under `fields`, only the roles the items really carry: `status` for a kanban, `start`
              (and `end`) for a calendar — a day, or `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM` where the time matters, which
              the week and the day place by the clock — `previous` for a tree, `previous` or `parent` for a
              sequence, the dates with `previous` or `parent` for a gantt, `parent` to nest items in
              the gantt and the sequence. A role not named is a view that cannot be offered — the
              right answer for items that have no dates, which the sequence lays out all the same.
          - key: views
            label: Pick the views
            after: [roles]
            detail: |
              Under `views`, one entry per way of looking: a `key`, a `kind`, a `label`, and a
              `filter` for what that view leaves out — `[{field: status, op: not_equals, value:
              Done}]` for the open work — with a `sort` and a `limit` where they help. A page is
              `kind: page` with a `template` over `items` — `{% for item in items %}` and the items'
              own keys — and the `partials` it includes. Leave `views` out to get one of every kind
              the roles allow.
          - key: columns
            label: Order the columns
            after: [views]
            detail: |
              `columns` lists the statuses in the order the kanban shows them; leave it out and the
              values found are the columns, first seen first.
          - key: check
            label: Check it
            after: [columns, content]
            detail: |
              `studio-check new.views` — an id used twice, a `previous` or `parent` to no item, a date
              that is not one, a status that is no column, a view whose roles are missing, a clause
              with an op outside the vocabulary, a cycle, anything wrong in an item's document: each
              is a line naming the view or the item, and the summary names the views the file offers
              with what each hides.
